    "title": "How we know what happened",
    "contentNote": "Content note: this card names genocide, apartheid and other mass violence.",
    "key": "We can say what happened when courts, investigators and survivors agree on the evidence, and we can say who found it.",
    "body": "Genocide, apartheid and occupation are not only opinions. Courts hear evidence and give rulings. Independent investigators publish findings. Survivors give accounts that are checked against records. A fair account says who found what: a court's ruling, an investigators' report, an organization's conclusion. Some questions are still before a court. We can say what has been found, and we can also say plainly where we stand.",
    "tryIt": "When you read a claim about a war or a state, look for who found it: a court, a commission, researchers, or someone with a stake. Ask what each would count as evidence.",
